{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when the ark set forward.</b> There are among our Sages of blessed memory who said (Shabbat 115b–116a) that these two verses constitute a book in their own right, and therefore they count seven books in the Torah. And should the matter surprise you — for after all the essence of the Torah was given for its commandments, and in this book there is no hint of any commandment — I will answer my portion and say that for the commandment of procreation He made a separate book, for on this commandment depends the perpetuation of the world and it brings about the indwelling of the Shekhinah, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Yevamot 63b): whoever does not engage in procreation causes the Shekhinah to withdraw from Israel, as it says: ״וּבְנֻחֹה יֹאמַר שׁוּבָה ה׳ רִבְבוֹת אַלְפֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — this teaches that the Shekhinah does not dwell among fewer than two myriads and two thousands of Israel. Now Israel were twenty-two thousand less one — and this one who did not engage in procreation, it turns out that he causes the Shekhinah to withdraw from Israel. And this is the first commandment with which the first man was charged, for immediately after it says (Genesis 1:27): ״וַיִּבְרָא אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָאָדָם״ etc., it is written (ibid. 1:28): ״וַיֹּאמֶר לָהֶם פְּרוּ וּרְבוּ״. Thus He made a special book out of this commandment, to declare that the indwelling of the Shekhinah in Israel requires twenty-two thousand. And consequently every man of Israel will know to be scrupulous in the commandment of procreation to fill the number. And a great proof for our words is from the portion of the complainers, which adjoins it, as will be explained immediately below."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:32.273259505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}